# Introduction

Hello, I am Ananth Narayan, PhD student from [Indian Institute of Technology Madras](https://www.iitm.ac.in/), predominently working on Electrohydrodynamics (EHD), multiphase, phase change with the idea of enhancing the heat transfer in mini/ micro channels. 

I have validated EHD solvers (unipolar and bipolar) in OpenFOAM for single phase, incompressible, laminar flow. Upon subsequent valdiation (in comparison with analytical solution [LopezHerrera2011](#LopezHerrera2011)) for multiphase flow with EHD it was revealed that OpenFOAM's isoAdvector and MULES were found not to capture the dynamics especially domination of spurious currents under EHD cases. Further trying with other available OpenFOAM solvers such as isoCLSVoF, sCLSVoF etc., it was decided that OpenFOAM's method were not accurate. Thus after spending 1 month in OpenFOAM's multiphase solvers, decided to switch to Basilisk.

Upon discussion with a senior [Rahul Subburaj](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=3z7NqpsAAAAJ&hl=en), understanding the key differences between Basilisk and OpenFOAM, I have decided to check out Basilisk and the test case written by López-Herrera, J.M. was helpful and now the [taylor.c](https://basilisk.fr/src/test/taylor.c) code has been modified and thus bubble deformation for various electric capillary number (CaE) is validated. 

I am happy to involve in discussion and you can contact me via [email](mailto:am24d038@smail.iitm.ac.in). Special thanks to [Edoardo Cipriano](https://basilisk.fr/sandbox/ecipriano/) for discussions related to Basilisk.

